      <div class="ehc-scenario-header">
        <span class="ehc-tag">Scenario 1</span>
        <strong class="ehc-scenario-name">Maria, Age 71 — Single, Just Under the Income Limit</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="ehc-scenario-desc">Maria receives $1,380/month Social Security and $320/month from a small pension — total $1,700/month. Her countable assets are $8,400 in a savings account (her home is excluded). She was unsure whether she qualified.</p>
      <table class="ehc-scenario-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Total monthly income</td><td>$1,700</td></tr>
          <tr class="ehc-row-alt"><td>Countable assets</td><td>$8,400</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2026 single income limit</td><td>~$1,985/month</td></tr>
          <tr class="ehc-row-alt"><td>2026 single asset limit</td><td>$17,220</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Eligibility result</td><td class="ehc-yes">✅ Full Extra Help — Qualifies</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="ehc-result-block">
        <span class="ehc-result-highlight">✅ Saves ~$4,800/Year — Copays Drop From $47 to $4.90 Per Generic</span>
        Maria was paying $47 per generic fill and $5.20/month Part D premium. With Full Extra Help: $0 deductible, $0 Part D premium (benchmark plan), $4.90 generic copay. On 4 monthly prescriptions: annual savings of ~$4,800.
      </div>
      <p class="ehc-action"><strong>Key:</strong> Maria's home was worth $240,000 but was completely excluded from the asset calculation. She nearly self-disqualified by counting it. Check our <a href="/tools/medicare-donut-hole-calculator/" class="ehc-inline-link">Medicare Drug Coverage Gap Calculator</a> to see how Extra Help interacts with the Part D catastrophic threshold.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ehc-scenario-card">
      <div class="ehc-scenario-header">
        <span class="ehc-tag">Scenario 2</span>
        <strong class="ehc-scenario-name">Robert &amp; Helen, Ages 73 &amp; 71 — Married Couple, Brand-Name Drugs</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="ehc-scenario-desc">Robert and Helen receive combined Social Security of $2,400/month and have $28,000 in a joint savings account. Robert takes three brand-name medications that previously cost $187/month combined.</p>
      <table class="ehc-scenario-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Combined monthly income</td><td>$2,400</td></tr>
          <tr class="ehc-row-alt"><td>Countable assets (joint)</td><td>$28,000</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2026 married income limit</td><td>~$2,685/month</td></tr>
          <tr class="ehc-row-alt"><td>2026 married asset limit</td><td>$34,360</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Eligibility result</td><td class="ehc-yes">✅ Full Extra Help — Both Qualify</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="ehc-result-block">
        <span class="ehc-result-highlight">✅ Robert's Brand-Name Copays Capped at $12.15 Each — Saves $151/Month</span>
        Robert's 3 brand-name drugs: previously $62/each = $186/month. Under Full Extra Help, each is capped at $12.15 = $36.45/month. Monthly savings: $149.55. Annual savings on drugs alone: $1,794 — plus $0 Part D deductible.
      </div>
      <p class="ehc-action"><strong>Key:</strong> The married asset limit of $34,360 is more than double the single limit — couples often have more room than they expect. If Robert later qualifies for a <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ehc-inline-link">Medicare Savings Program</a>, Extra Help status becomes automatic and permanent while MSP continues.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ehc-scenario-card">
      <div class="ehc-scenario-header">
        <span class="ehc-tag">Scenario 3</span>
        <strong class="ehc-scenario-name">Dorothy, Age 68 — Slightly Over Income Limit, But Qualifies for MSP</strong>
      </div>
      <p class="ehc-scenario-desc">Dorothy's Social Security is $2,200/month — $215 above the single Extra Help income limit. She doesn't qualify for Extra Help directly. But the screener surfaces an alternative.</p>
      <table class="ehc-scenario-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Input</th><th>Value</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Monthly income</td><td>$2,200</td></tr>
          <tr class="ehc-row-alt"><td>2026 single income limit</td><td>~$1,985</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Extra Help eligibility</td><td class="ehc-no">❌ Does not qualify</td></tr>
          <tr class="ehc-row-alt"><td>SLMB income limit (2026)</td><td>~$2,270/month</td></tr>
          <tr><td>SLMB eligibility</td><td class="ehc-yes">✅ Qualifies — Part B premium paid ($185/mo)</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <div class="ehc-result-block">
        <span class="ehc-result-highlight">✅ $2,220/Year in Part B Premium Savings Found Through MSP — Even Though Extra Help Denied</span>
        Dorothy doesn't get Extra Help, but our <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ehc-inline-link">Medicare Savings Programs Estimator</a> shows she qualifies for SLMB — which pays her entire $185/month Part B premium. Annual value: $2,220. Apply through her state Medicaid office, not SSA.
      </div>
      <p class="ehc-action"><strong>Key:</strong> A denial from Extra Help doesn't mean no help exists. Medicare Savings Programs have different (and sometimes higher) income limits. Always screen for MSPs separately using our <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ehc-inline-link">Medicare Savings Programs Estimator</a>.</p>
    </div>

    <p class="ehc-disclaimer">These are representative examples using official 2026 SSA thresholds. Only SSA makes final eligibility determinations. Income and asset limits are updated annually. Apply at ssa.gov/extrahelp for an official determination.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ELEMENT 6 — PITFALLS -->
  <section class="ehc-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Extra Help Mistakes — And How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Not Knowing You Already Qualify Automatically</h3>
    <p>If you receive full Medicaid benefits, SSI, or are enrolled in a Medicare Savings Program (QMB, SLMB, or QI), you automatically qualify for Full Extra Help. SSA receives a notification from your state and typically enrolls you without any action on your part. Thousands of seniors who qualify this way still pay unnecessary Part D premiums and copays because they don't know their automatic status.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to call 1-800-MEDICARE (1-800-633-4227) and ask: "Do I currently have Extra Help or LIS status on my Medicare account?" If you qualify through Medicaid or an MSP but aren't receiving LIS benefits, SSA can correct this immediately. Check whether you have an MSP using our <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ehc-inline-link">Medicare Savings Programs Estimator</a>.</p>

    <h3>Counting Your Primary Home as a Countable Asset</h3>
    <p>SSA's Extra Help asset limits specifically exclude your primary residence — regardless of its value. A senior with a $400,000 home but only $12,000 in savings fully qualifies for Extra Help on the asset test. Yet many people look at their home equity and conclude they "have too much" and never apply.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to count only liquid financial assets: cash, bank accounts, stocks, bonds, and retirement account balances. Exclude: primary home, one vehicle (any value), personal belongings, and up to $1,500 per person set aside for burial. The official exclusion list is at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/benefits/medicare/prescriptionhelp.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">SSA Extra Help Program Page</a>.</p>

    <h3>Missing the Annual Re-Verification Notice From SSA</h3>
    <p>Extra Help is not a one-time approval. SSA sends a yellow re-verification notice each year — typically in September or October — asking you to confirm your income and assets haven't changed significantly. Many seniors miss this notice, fail to respond, and are disenrolled from Extra Help for the following year without realizing it.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to watch for the yellow SSA mailing each fall and respond promptly. If your income and assets haven't changed significantly, the form takes about 10 minutes to complete. If you missed last year's notice and lost Extra Help, you can re-apply immediately at <a href="https://www.ssa.gov/extrahelp" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">ssa.gov/extrahelp</a> — there is no waiting period for re-application.</p>

    <h3>Self-Disqualifying When Slightly Over the Income Limit</h3>
    <p>The Extra Help income limits are based on <em>countable</em> income — not your full gross income. SSA allows deductions for certain excluded income types. Additionally, if your income is only marginally above the Full Extra Help limit, you may qualify for Partial Extra Help, which still provides substantial savings. And if you're above both thresholds, a <a href="/tools/medicare-savings-estimator/" class="ehc-inline-link">Medicare Savings Program</a> often covers ground Extra Help doesn't reach.</p>
    <p><strong>The fix is</strong> to let this calculator run the actual numbers before concluding you don't qualify. Never self-disqualify based on a rough estimate. If borderline, always apply and let SSA make the determination. The application has no cost and no negative consequence if denied.</p>
  </section>

